te":"no"},{"name":"Sean Edwards","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10756","person_slug":"sean-edwards","vote":"no"},{"name":"Simon Birmingham","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10044","person_slug":"simon-birmingham","vote":"no"},{"name":"Stephen Conroy","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10142","person_slug":"stephen-conroy","vote":"no"},{"name":"Stephen Parry","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10507","person_slug":"stephen-parry","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sue Boyce","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10062","person_slug":"sue-boyce","vote":"no"},{"name":"Trish Crossin","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10161","person_slug":"trish-crossin","vote":"no"},{"name":"Ursula Stephens","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10609","person_slug":"ursula-stephens","vote":"no"}],"name":"Motions — Suspension of Standing Orders","noes":49,"question":"Bob Brown I move: That so much of standing orders be suspended as would prevent me making a statement. Mr President, I do that because what we have now before the Senate is a request, which you have granted, from Senator Abetz via Senator Kroger—which of course mentions Senator Milne on the way—to have me referred to the Committee of Privileges regarding a donation which came to the Australian Greens, and which I have stated on the public record was in no way an influence on me or a donation to me as a person. And then consequent to that were matters raised regarding the Triabunna woodchip mill sale five months ago. The President has ruled that the donation is a matter which should be examined by the Committee of Privileges and that a matter having been raised five months ago makes it a further matter that should be examined by the Committee of Privileges. However, in the case of Senator Boswell, a donation directly to him, on the record, which was 12 months ago, does not receive the same ruling from the President. Here we have a President who is making contradictory rulings when it comes to a National Party senator as against a Greens senator. What we have in the President's ruling— Ian Macdonald Mr Deputy President, I rise on a point of order on the basis of that being a reflection on a decision of the President. Stephen Parry I do not think there is a point of order, Senator Macdonald. Senator Brown, you have the call. Bob Brown Thank you, Mr Deputy President. What we have is a double standard coming from the chair— Opposition senators interjecting— Stephen Parry Order on my left! Bob Brown The members opposite do not like that, but the reality is—and let me make this very clear—that we now have a ruling from the chair, directed at me, regarding a donation and a consequent referral to matters in the Senate, which does not apply, according to the President, to Senator Boswell or to a series of other senators who have gained monetary advan­tage or sought donations ","result":"negative","slug":"division-federal-senate-2207","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2011-11-25/7"}
